<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="128" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Gate POST /api/office/todo on its REGARDING record — the only live unauthorized WRITE on the Office surface</title>
    <phase>Register-driven (authored 2026-09-29, session 26; GitHub #1022, from the task 120 census)</phase>
    <gate>startable</gate>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Adds an authorization filter to a live write path and changes the response ordering a shipped add-in observes.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>The filter's reach is a silent-failure surface: attaching it without teaching ExtractTargetEntity the request type yields a filter that is present, credited by the arch guard, and does nothing.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>none</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>false</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Touches OfficeEndpoints.cs, EntityAccessFilter.cs and the arch-test waiver list, all of which tasks 126/127 just changed.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>126, 127</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, auth, security</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-3 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>GitHub #1022, filed by task 120's route census. Ranked #3 of five live holes by the session-26 waiver audit — the only WRITE.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>A caller can attach a to-do to a record they have no right to touch, through the app identity. Make the route check the record it writes against.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — Office endpoints and the entity-access filter.</role>

  <goal>POST /api/office/todo authorizes the caller against the regarding record before writing, without breaking the legitimate no-regarding (personal to-do) case.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>Unlike #1021 and #1020, this one IS a filter fix — `/api/office/save` carries
`.AddEntityAccessFilter()` and this route simply never got it. It had rate limiting, idempotency and
`OfficeAuthFilter` (authentication only), and nothing verifying access to the caller-named regarding
record before writing through the app identity.

Distinct from `/quickcreate` (Permanent waiver): that route CREATES the record, so there is no
pre-existing resource to authorize. Here the regarding record already exists.

⚠️ The regarding pair is OPTIONAL on `CreateTodoRequest` and the handler validates only `Name`, so a
to-do with no regarding record is legitimate — and for it the filter's pass-through is correct rather
than a gap. That is the same branch #1025 concerns.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/Office/OfficeEndpoints.cs</file>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/Filters/EntityAccessFilter.cs</file>
      <file role="modify">tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint>Teach `ExtractTargetEntity` the request shape in the SAME change that attaches the filter — a filter whose extractor returns null passes through and authorizes nothing.</constraint>
    <constraint>Verify type-name compatibility BEFORE attaching: `EntityAccessFilter` rejects an unknown entity type with 400, so a regarding type accepted by the route but missing from the filter's map would break working requests.</constraint>
    <constraint>Do NOT break the no-regarding case — a to-do with no regarding record is a legitimate personal to-do with nothing to authorize against.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step>Confirm the route lacks the filter and that `/save` has it.</step>
    <step>Check every regarding type the route accepts resolves in the filter's map.</step>
    <step>Extend `ExtractTargetEntity`, attach the filter, and update the route's own comments.</step>
    <step>Resolve the waiver; add guards and prove they bite.</step>
    <step>Close #1022.</step>
  </steps>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>The route carries `.AddEntityAccessFilter()` and the filter can see `CreateTodoRequest`.</criterion>
    <criterion>Every regarding type the route accepts is resolvable by the filter — pinned by a test.</criterion>
    <criterion>The no-regarding (personal to-do) case still works.</criterion>
    <criterion>The waiver is resolved; BFF unit + NetArchTest green.</criterion>
    <criterion>#1022 closed with the outcome recorded.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Authored 2026-09-29 (session 26) after tasks 126/127. The smallest of the three by code volume and the one with the most room to look done while doing nothing.</notes>

  <execution>
    <outcome>Completed 2026-09-29. `ExtractTargetEntity` now projects `CreateTodoRequest`'s
    `RegardingEntityType`/`RegardingRecordId` into a `SaveEntityReference`, and the route carries
    `.AddEntityAccessFilter()`. The waiver was DELETED rather than made Permanent — with a real filter
    attached, Rule A credits the route and no waiver is needed, which is the outcome to prefer: a waiver
    is a note explaining why the mechanical check cannot see a control. ArchTests pass with the entry
    gone, which is the strongest available confirmation that the guard sees it. BFF unit 12,682 pass /
    0 fail; NetArchTest 330/330.</outcome>

    <finding>🔴 COMPATIBILITY WAS A REAL RISK AND WAS CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED. `EntityAccessFilter` rejects
    an unknown entity type with 400, so a regarding type the route accepts but the filter cannot resolve
    would have converted working requests into bad ones — a gate that breaks the feature instead of
    guarding it. `OfficeService._todoRegardingMap` accepts only Matter/Project/Invoice, all present in
    `EntitySetByType`, and that dictionary is `OrdinalIgnoreCase`, so the capitalised forms the add-in
    sends resolve against lowercase keys. Pinned by a test so a fourth type cannot be added to one map
    and not the other.</finding>

    <finding>ADDING THE FILTER CHANGED RESPONSE ORDERING, and a contract test caught it: authorization
    now runs BEFORE shape validation, so a request naming an inaccessible regarding record is denied
    rather than told its payload is malformed. That ordering is CORRECT — an unauthorized caller should
    not learn whether their body was well-formed — so the test was repointed rather than the behaviour
    changed. It now omits the regarding record, exercising name validation in isolation via the
    legitimate pass-through, and a NEW test pins the ordering by asserting that a body which is invalid
    AND unauthorized returns the authorization outcome.</finding>

    <finding>The filter's reach is its silent-failure surface. `ExtractTargetEntity` returns null for an
    unrecognised request shape, and a null target makes the filter PASS THROUGH — so attaching the filter
    without extending that method yields a filter that is present, credited by Rule A, and authorizes
    nothing. Verified to bite by removing the attachment and watching the guard fail. Documented at the
    method itself so the next route to be gated does not repeat it.</finding>
  </execution>

  <superseded-at-merge date="2026-09-30">
    SUPERSEDED by spaarkeai-word-add-in-r1 task 064, whose TodoSourceAccessFilter gates ALL FOUR
    caller-supplied ids on POST /api/office/todo (regardingRecordId, documentId, communicationId,
    assignedToContactId) where this task gated only the regarding id — and 064 also caught a leak this task
    missed: CoreAncestorResolver reads the regarding target app-only to stamp its parent matter/project, so an
    ungated id let a caller harvest that record's core ancestor. The merge kept 064. Removed at merge: this
    task's .AddEntityAccessFilter() on /todo, the CreateTodoRequest branch in EntityAccessFilter.ExtractTargetEntity
    (dead once the route stopped using the filter; the "this method is the filter's reach" warning was KEPT, since
    it holds for /save), TodoRegardingAuthorizationGuardTests, and the IsDeniedBeforeValidation contract test.
    TodoSourceAccessFilter (and master's QuickCreateSourceAccessFilter) were added to the census's credited
    allow-list, since neither name contains "Authorization" and Rule A could not see them.

    ⚠️ A MISS WORTH KEEPING ON RECORD: this task attached a filter enforcing entity.associate_document, which
    OperationAccessPolicy maps to AppendTo — and no end-user role granted AppendTo until the owner granted it to
    "Spaarke Office Add In User" on 2026-09-29. Had that grant not landed, /todo would have returned 403 to every
    non-admin user. The task checked type-name compatibility (can the filter resolve the entity type) and never
    checked rights compatibility (does any end-user role hold the right it demands). Both dimensions must be
    checked before attaching an authorization filter.
  </superseded-at-merge>
</task>
